  "worked_solution": "Let's check each statement.\n\nIf $P$ is between $Q$ and $R$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =PQ+PR \\\\ 13 & =7+PR \\\\ 13-7 & =PR \\\\ PR & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182164/assets/d7396a24e0e8884195184a4fca2b4e0fd9cfc2462249c7dc3a0c66901d062c5f.png)\n\nTherefore, $P$ can lie between $Q$ and $R,$ so statement I is false.\n\nIf $Q$ is between $P$ and $R$, then again, by the segments addition postulate, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =PQ+QR \\\\  & =7+13 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182164/assets/2dc899f0b5d08bf4a5b5e1833e62e4a3290c8da83dbd376ed51d185c1ed08e94.png)\n\nTherefore, $Q$ can lie between $P$ and $R,$ so statement II is false.\n\nIf $R$ is between $P$ and $Q$, then, by the segments addition postulate, we would have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =PR+QR \\\\ 7 & =PR+13 \\\\ PR & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis can't happen since the length of a segment can't be negative. \n\nTherefore, $R$ cannot lie between $P$ and $Q,$ so statement III is true.",
